{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{6}a + \\log_{6}b + \\log_{6}c = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can combine the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{6}(abc) = 6\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\nabc = 6^6\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) form an increasing geometric sequence, we can express them as \\(a = x\\), \\(b = xr\\), and \\(c = xr^2\\) where \\(r\\) is the common ratio. Substituting these into the equation \\(abc = 6^6\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx \\cdot xr \\cdot xr^2 = 6^6\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\n\\[\nx^3 r^3 = 6^6\n\\]\n\nTaking the cube root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nxr = 6^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\nb = 36\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) such that \\(b - a\\) is the square of an integer. Let \\(a = \\frac{36}{r}\\) and \\(c = 36r\\). We know that \\(b - a = 36 - \\frac{36}{r}\\) must be a perfect square. Let:\n\n\\[\n36 - \\frac{36}{r} = k^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer. Rearranging the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n36\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{r}\\right) = k^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n36 - \\frac{36}{r} = k^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(r - 1)}{r} = k^2\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1050,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1191,448,279,2661,23606,1447,59,9640,59,839,15159,21,92,64,488,1124,839,15159,21,92,65,488,1124,839,15159,21,92,66,284,220,21,198,59,2533,16429,279,5888,315,89936,1011,11,582,646,15963,279,3793,389,279,2115,24413,3108,1447,59,9640,59,839,15159,21,25547,13683,8,284,220,21,198,59,2533,1986,23945,1447,59,9640,13683,284,220,21,61,21,198,59,2533,12549,17767,64,59,701,17767,65,59,701,323,17767,66,57758,1352,458,7703,52484,8500,11,582,646,3158,1105,438,17767,64,284,856,59,701,17767,65,284,38670,59,701,323,17767,66,284,38670,61,17,57758,1380,17767,81,57758,374,279,4185,11341,13,3719,36