:{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,2132,374,2661,429,57960,839,15159,21,92,64,488,1124,839,15159,21,92,65,488,1124,839,15159,21,92,66,284,220,21,54876,1380,400,64,54876,400,65,54876,323,400,66,3,525,6785,25780,429,1352,458,7703,52484,8500,323,400,65,481,264,3,374,279,9334,315,458,7546,13,7379,400,64,488,293,488,272,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIt is given that $\\log_{6}a + \\log_{6}b + \\log_{6}c = 6$, where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are positive integers that form an increasing geometric sequence and $b - a$ is the square of an integer. Find $a + b + c$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"9392a1687ef438ff2a997e95f0840ccd7d60dec64f75a253a125c5dfcdd6fbe7"},"seconds":26.200436115264893,"seed":686334421633970916,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start with the given equation:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{6}a + \\log_{6}b + \\log_{6}c = 6\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can combine the terms on the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\log_{6}(abc) = 6\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\nabc = 6^6\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) form an increasing geometric sequence, we can express them as \\(a = x\\), \\(b = xr\\), and \\(c = xr^2\\) where \\(r\\) is the common ratio. Substituting these into the equation \\(abc = 6^6\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx \\cdot xr \\cdot xr^2 = 6^6\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\n\\[\nx^3 r^3 = 6^6\n\\]\n\nTaking the cube root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nxr = 6^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\n\\[\nb = 36\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(a\\) and \\(c\\) such that \\(b - a\\) is the square of an integer. Let \\(a = \\frac{36}{r}\\) and \\(c = 36r\\). We know that \\(b - a = 36 - \\frac{36}{r}\\) must be a perfect square. Let:\n\n\\[\n36 - \\frac{36}{r} = k^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer. Rearranging the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n36\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{r}\\right) = k^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n36 - \\frac{36}{r} = k^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(r - 1)}{r} = k^2\n\\]\n\nSince \\(36 = 2^2 \\cdot 3^2\\), \\(r\\) must be such that \\(\\frac{r - 1}{r}\\) simplifies to a fraction whose product with \\(36\\) results in a perfect square. Testing possible values for \\(r\\):\n\n1. If \\(r = 2\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(2 - 1)}{2} = \\frac{36}{2} = 18 \\quad (\\text{not a perfect square})\n\\]\n\n2. If \\(r = 3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(3 - 1)}{3} = \\frac{72}{3} = 24 \\quad (\\text{not a perfect square})\n\\]\n\n3. If \\(r = 4\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(4 - 1)}{4} = \\frac{108}{4} = 27 \\quad (\\text{not a perfect square})\n\\]\n\n4. If \\(r = 5\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(5 - 1)}{5} = \\frac{144}{5} = 28.8 \\quad (\\text{not an integer})\n\\]\n\n5. If \\(r = 6\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(6 - 1)}{6} = \\frac{180}{6} = 30 \\quad (\\text{not a perfect square})\n\\]\n\n6. If \\(r = 9\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(9 - 1)}{9} = \\frac{288}{9} = 32 \\quad (\\text{not a perfect square})\n\\]\n\n7. If \\(r = 10\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(10 - 1)}{10} = \\frac{324}{10} = 32.4 \\quad (\\text{not an integer})\n\\]\n\n8. If \\(r = 18\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{36(18 - 1)}{18} = \\frac{648}{18} = 36 = 6^2 \\quad (\\text{a perfect square})\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(r = 18\\). Substituting \\(r = 18\\):\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{36}{18} = 2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nb = 36\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc = 36 \\cdot 18 = 648\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\(a + b + c\\):\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 2 + 36 + 648 = 686\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{686}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{686}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:849"}
